Yes—those blue circles are Assertion nodes, and the text inside them is a relationship predicate. Your interpretation is correct.
The current model stores the fact twice:
(Process)-[:OPERATES_IN]->(Canada)
And an audit representation:
(Assertion: "OPERATES_IN → Canada")-[:SUBJECT]->(Process)
(Assertion)-[:OBJECT]->(Canada)
(Assertion)-[:EVIDENCED_BY]->(DocumentVersion)
The assertion layer records which documents support the relationship, confidence, and version status. It is useful for deep auditability, but it makes the graph noisy and confusing for this POC.
To see everything except technical assertion nodes:
MATCH path=(source)-[relationship]->(target)
WHERE NOT source:Assertion
  AND NOT target:Assertion
RETURN path
LIMIT 500;
For the clean business graph:
MATCH path=(process:Process)
  -[:OPERATES_IN|OWNED_BY|GOVERNED_BY|USES_SYSTEM]->(entity)
RETURN path;
My recommendation, based on your objective, is to remove Assertion nodes entirely and store provenance directly on the business relationship:
(Process)-[:OPERATES_IN {
    confidence: 1.0,
    evidence_count: 4,
    document_version_ids: [...],
    supporting_chunk_ids: [...]
}]->(Canada)
That would leave only real enterprise entities as nodes and real business connections as relationships. The trade-off is that provenance becomes relationship properties instead of a separately traversable audit layer. For this demonstration, that is clearer and better aligned with your stated model.
